-- The BECAUSE payload of `#ASSERT REFUSED` is a LITERAL, not an expression --
-- exactly as REFUSE's own message is (see `refuse-computed-message.l4`). The
-- grammar admits only a literal there, so a computed message is a PARSE error.
--
-- That is deliberate, and it is not merely a simplification. The directive
-- exists to PIN a refusal's reason, and the comparison is decided statically,
-- without evaluating the payload. A payload the evaluator could not read
-- statically would degrade to "this directive constrains nothing" -- an
-- assertion that reports `satisfied` for ANY refusal reason, which is the one
-- outcome a message-pinning directive must never produce.

GIVETH A NUMBER
`nope` MEANS REFUSE "the real reason"

GIVETH A STRING
`wrong reason` MEANS "a totally different reason"

#ASSERT REFUSED `nope` BECAUSE `wrong reason`
